A||N||7||87ECO5||Strange behavior from car since new tires were installed||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||07-17-2000||01:05 PM||||Up until last weekend, my '87 coupe was running on an old set of 195/70R14 Goodyear Eagle ST tires.  On Saturday, I installed a "new" set of 215/60R14 Daytona Radial S/Rs.  I say "new" because they are actually used tires but have very little mileage on them.  Here's the thread that shows photos:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002946.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002946.html</A> <P>When I had them mounted on my new rims, the guys doing the job didn't say anything about the tires being in bad shape or that they were hard to balance.<P>Anyway, got a question about something it's doing now that I put the new rubber on it.  I'm sure it does it constantly but I'm noticing it more at speeds above 35 miles per hour.  When I'm accelerating and I push the clutch in to shift gears, the nose of the car moves slightly to the left.  Then, when I release the clutch, it moves back to the right.  Now, since the shifts are happening quickly, those movements to the left and back also happen quickly so the motion feels more like I moved the steering wheel quickly to the left and back to center (but I'm sure I'm not really moving the steering wheel).  It's nothing major - it's just a minor movement but I certainly notice it.  Any ideas?<P>fiero56 has experienced this movement so maybe he can provide additional information.<P>Oh, and BTW, the car does pull <B><I>slightly</I></B> to the left while driving.  Again, nothing major but enough to be noticeable.  It's almost like the pull is reduced while accelerating then increased once acceleration stops.<P>Does this make sense?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||07-17-2000||01:41 PM||||the new tires likely just made it more obvious but aren't the root cause. It's been doing that right allong to some extent. It's likely the cradle bushings and/or lower control arm bushings. This has been covered allot but not recently.<P>It's a torque reaction problem. It's mostly caused by the dog bone being mounted off center to the body and the engine cradle being in rubber. When the engine load suddenly changes it shifts the right side of the craddle for and aft more than the left side litterally turning the whole rear of the car like waggon wheels. It's one of the reasons many of us have put in polly or even solid cradle bushings.<P>------------------<BR>Lake Superior? Is that what I stepped in? Sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-17-2000||01:42 PM||||It sounds like one or 2 of the tires are on the wrong side. Radial tires "wear" into the sides they were originally mounted on. If you bought them used and they were not marked as to which came from the left side and right side of the original car, it will cause vibrations and premature wear. It can throw off your alignment a little too. Especially if the car they came off of had it's alignment off and wore the tire slightly. You could try swapping tires from left to right and see which set (front or back) or both is causing the problem. Try swapping the back first since you notice it more so during acceleration/decceleration. Normally you can only rotate radial tires from front to back not left to right.<BR>The pulling to the left may require an alignment if the swapping doesn't fix it.<P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-17-2000||07:49 PM||||Hey Ogre,<P>I'm not sure I follow your reponse. The engine and tranny may shift around, but the A arms and spindles and everything stay stationary and in alignement, don't they? How does the shift in engine position affect the mounting points of the wheels/tires. I'm curious about that, because I experience some slight twisting when I get on it / off it really hard. I always just sort of attributed it to the cradle shifting, in which case solid mounts would seem to make it worse.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||07-17-2000||09:51 PM||||I'll take a stab at it, until Ogre gets here. The rear suspesnsion on all Fieros is mounted directly to the cradle. The only thing touching the car are the tops of the struts. Everything in back(lower control arms for '84-'87, tri-link for '88) is mounted to the cradle. The cradle is basically a big steel square, that the engine, tranny, and suspension all mount to. This square then bolts to the frame of the car from below. '84-'87 are mounted with rubber bushings between the cradle and frame. In '88 they took out the bushings, so the cradle directly bolts to the frame.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||07-17-2000||10:45 PM||||Those cradle mounts aren't going to be moving around much until you really get into a hard corner unless they're falling out of the car,sounds like balljoints,shocks,or as TheOgre said,bushings on the control arms  worn out.<BR>The squatier,more "square"tires are going to bring out the worst in any suspension,whether it be worn parts,or alignment,don't forget old wheel bearings either;most feel great when on a jack,yet if the wheel is tugged on with the weight of the car,they'll clunk around a bit from wear.<BR>The tire's wear pattern (assigned by a completely different car) will make for some strange effects too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||PJ||07-17-2000||11:57 PM||||One more opinion and yeah it makes sense. When you dip the clutch or deaccelerate weigh tranfers to the front of the car. Try swapping the front wheels and see if the symptoms move with them.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black||||reg||1
Z||000007||2M4 Dale||07-18-2000||10:04 AM||||Scott , I noticed this kind of problem after I installed the new Goodyear GTII's on my coupe last year. The problem went away after I installed the polly cradle bushings and ultimate dogbone. You may want to have it realigned too.||||reg||1
